# This file is used for Git repositories to specify intentionally untracked files that Git should ignore. 
# If you are not using git, you can delete this file. For more information see: https://git-scm.com/docs/gitignore
# For useful gitignore templates see: https://github.com/github/gitignore
# Salesforce cache
.sf/
.sfdx/
.localdevserver/
deploy-options.json
# LWC VSCode autocomplete
**/lwc/jsconfig.json
# LWC Jest coverage reports
coverage/
# Logs
logs
*.log
npm-debug.log*
yarn-debug.log*
yarn-error.log*
# Dependency directories
node_modules/
# ApexPMD cache
.pmdCache
# Eslint cache
.eslintcache
# MacOS system files
.DS_Store
# Windows system files
Thumbs.db
ehthumbs.db
[Dd]esktop.ini
$RECYCLE.BIN/
# Local environment variables
.env
# Python Salesforce Functions
**/__pycache__/
**/.venv/
**/venv/

# Local-only patterns live in .git/info/exclude, which is not published. A
# .gitignore that lists private filenames advertises what it is hiding.

# The HanseWatt agent's action classes. Deleted on 2026-08-29 because they call a
# service layer that exists in no copy of this repository - see force-app/README.md.
# They are listed here because regenerating that org's report RETRIEVES metadata
# and writes these back to disk, so `git add -A` re-committed them once already.
force-app/main/default/classes/HW*Action.cls
force-app/main/default/classes/HW*Action.cls-meta.xml

# Default --out scratch. Never an artifact: reports/ holds the published ones,
# and a stray run here once put a live org identifier into a tracked file.
blast_radius/report.md
blast_radius/report.html

# reports/ belongs entirely to whoever runs this. Nothing of mine lives there:
# the two published examples moved to examples/, because a stranger who clones
# this and runs it should find ONE report in that folder - their own - not eight
# files from two orgs they have never heard of, with theirs as the ninth.
#
# It is also the safe default: a report carries your org's usernames and object
# names, and a tracked output file is exactly how a live org identifier got
# committed into this repository once already.
reports/

# Scratch clones made while rehearsing the published command.
clonetest/
abr-test/

# verify_deterministic.py writes these two pairs beside the repo root. Only the
# .md half was excluded, so a `git add -A` would have swept the .html half in -
# the sweep that has already committed a gitlink and three org classes tonight.
_det_*

# Retrieved Apex lands here, because that is where `sf project retrieve start`
# writes. A `git add -A` after a live run has now committed other orgs' source
# THREE times in one night - a gitlink, three VoltStream classes, then seven
# HanseWatt services - each time into a repository that is about to be public.
#
# .gitignore does not affect files git already tracks, so every evidence class
# under force-app stays exactly as tracked as it was; only NEW arrivals are
# ignored. A genuinely new evidence class therefore needs `git add -f`, which is
# the right amount of friction for the one case that is deliberate.
force-app/main/default/classes/*.cls
force-app/main/default/classes/*.cls-meta.xml
force-app/main/default/flows/*.flow-meta.xml

# A retrieve writes localActions/<id>/<id>/{input,output}/schema.json under a
# planner bundle. Nothing reads them - the analyzer parses the <localActions>
# ELEMENT inside the bundle file - and they carry the longest paths here, which
# is what broke `git clone` on stock Windows. Removed once already; a `git add -A`
# after the next live run put them straight back, which is why this is a rule now
# and not a cleanup. The bundle files themselves stay tracked: tests read them.
force-app/main/default/genAiPlannerBundles/*/localActions/
